sus level_003 were also null for the registered hypotheses, with most signed estimates well below practical scale and intervals including null. The only interval-exclusive feature_048 result was opposite to the registered stronger-dependency hypothesis in outcome_009, but its lower bound remained below the practical threshold and multiplicity made it unsuitable for acceptance or validation. Tested interactions, including those stratified by feature_010, feature_015, and feature_051, did not produce robust subgroup-specific associations.\n\nSupported conclusions are therefore mainly negative and bounded: within this supplied dataset, the analyzed measured predictors and tested subgroups do not show scientifically meaningful differences in the screened CRISPR dependency outcomes under the stated practical threshold. This should not be interpreted as proof of causal absence, because the screen is observational, multiple, dependent on the supplied labels and coding, and not exhaustive of the predictor or outcome space.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains for the active feature_042 main-effect panel H291 through H302, the high-variance outcomes outcome_006 and outcome_014 where power is limited, rare categorical levels deliberately avoided because of small cells, untested continuous dose-response relationships, and higher-order interactions. External generalizability and biological interpretation also remain unknown.\n\nThe most informative next analysis is to complete the active feature_042 panel and apply the same acceptance rule: accept only an interval-exclusive signed effect at or near the outcome-specific 10% practical scale, preferably with independent validation for borderline or high-impact findings. If feature_042 is null, the investigation should move either to a small pre-specified follow-up panel for remaining high-prevalence categorical predictors, such as feature_028 or feature_036, or to close the broad univariate categorical screen and interpret the accumu