  "narrative": "No claim assessments are due, and the accepted set remains empty. The broad binary and categorical univariate screen has been largely negative: common binary predictors, high-prevalence categorical contrasts, rare-level contrasts, prior interactions, and eligibility-stratified contrasts have not produced robust interval-exclusive practical dependency-score differences, and independent validation failed for the most borderline candidates. The active feature_045 panel H315-H320 should still be treated as the highest-priority discovery completion if analysis capacity permits. The new proposals here are a focused transition away from marginal univariate mining toward continuous clinical or biomarker-like effect modification.\n\nThe registered hypotheses are interaction comparisons asking whether common binary predictors have differential dependency-score associations in high versus low subgroups defined by measured continuous features. The first panel tests feature_034=1 versus feature_034=0 across six low-dispersion core dependency outcomes, with feature_024 as a complete continuous candidate clinical modifier split at its observed median. The second panel tests feature_042=1 versus feature_042=0 across outcome_012, outcome_005, and outcome_015, restricted to nonmissing feature_061 and split at feature_061=54, an age-like continuous predictor. The third panel tests feature_060=1 versus feature_060=0 across the same three core outcomes, restricted to nonmissing feature_056 and split at feature_056=52, a continuous predictor with a plausible clinical or biomarker scale. These predictors were chosen because they are measured, continuous, reasonably prevalent after median splitting, and not covered by the prior categorical-only screen.\n\nFor all registered contrasts, direction -1 encodes the claim that the raw exposed-minus-comparator association, or raw interaction contrast, is negative; that is, the exposed category has a more negative dependency score relative to its comparator in the high subgroup than in the low subgroup. Because returned estimates are already signed, positive signed estimates would support the registered hypothesis, while negative signed estimates would represent opposite effect modification. Anticipated directions are set to zero because the marginal marker effects have generally been null or inconsistent, so no strong prior expectation about the sign of continuous effect modification is justified. Falsifiable alternatives include no modification, opposite modification, apparent modification caused by confounding among correlated predictors, missingness-driven selection, or random correlated multiple testing.\n\nThe practical benchmark remains a 10% or greater relative difference in dependency score. For the selected core outcomes, absolute means are near 0.25 and standard deviations near 0.18, so this corresponds roughly to a 0.025 absolute dependency-score-unit effect. For these interaction claims, acceptance should require a signed estimate supporting the registered direction whose interval excludes both zero and clinically negligible values around the 10% practical threshold. Interval-inclusive, subthreshold, opposite-direction, or unstable cell estimates should be treated as negative. All conclusions remain limited to the supplied observational dataset and do not establish causality, biological mechanism, or external generalizability. If the feature_045 panel and these continuous modifier panels are null, the investigation should conclude with a bounded negative result rather than further rare-level or marginal categorical mining.",
